// Practice quiz: inner and left joins
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters and treats colon, equals, hash, tilde and braces as syntax, so those are escaped.

$CATEGORY: sql-jdbc-foundations/crud-aggregates-and-joins/inner-and-left-joins

::o1 rows from an inner join::Author 1 has two books, author 2 has one book, and author 3 has none. How many rows does an INNER JOIN of authors to books on author_id return? {
=3#Correct. An inner join returns one row per matching pair, so author 1 appears twice and author 3 not at all.
~2#Not quite. Author 1 matches two books, so it contributes two rows.
~4#Not quite. Author 3 has no match, and an inner join drops unmatched rows.
~9#Not quite. That is every author paired with every book; a join keeps only pairs where ON is true.
}

::o1 the ON condition::books.author_id refers to authors.author_id. Which ON condition joins each book to its author? {
~ON books.book_id \= authors.author_id#Not quite. A book id and an author id only match by coincidence.
~ON books.title \= authors.name#Not quite. Matching text that merely looks similar does not follow the relationship.
=ON books.author_id \= authors.author_id#Correct. Join the foreign key to the key it references.
~ON books.author_id \= books.author_id#Not quite. That compares a column with itself, so every author pairs with every book.
}

::o2 rows from a left join::Same data\: author 1 has two books, author 2 one, author 3 none. How many rows does authors LEFT JOIN books return? {
~3#Not quite. The unmatched author 3 is kept by a left join.
~5#Not quite. Unmatched left rows appear once, not once per missing book.
~9#Not quite. A left join keeps matching pairs plus one row for each unmatched left row, not every pairing.
=4#Correct. A left join keeps every author\: three rows for the matched books plus one row for author 3.
}

::o2 where NULLs appear::In authors LEFT JOIN books, which columns are NULL on the row for an author with no books? {
~The authors columns#Not quite. The author row is the one being kept, so its columns hold their values.
=The books columns#Correct. The left row is kept, and the right-side columns it has no match for are filled with NULL.
~Every column#Not quite. Only the unmatched side is NULL-extended.
~None; the row is removed#Not quite. Removing it would be an inner join.
}

::o3 include customers who never ordered::A report must list every customer with any orders, including customers who have never ordered. Which join fits? {
~customers INNER JOIN orders#Not quite. That drops customers who have no order.
~orders LEFT JOIN customers#Not quite. That keeps every order; customers without orders still disappear.
~Two separate queries with no join#Not quite. The report needs each customer next to their orders.
=customers LEFT JOIN orders#Correct. A left join from customers keeps customers with no matching order.
}

::o3 only customers with orders::A report should list only customers who have placed at least one order, with each order. Which join fits? {
~customers LEFT JOIN orders#Not quite. That would also keep customers with no orders.
=customers INNER JOIN orders#Correct. An inner join keeps exactly the customers that have a matching order.
~customers LEFT JOIN orders with no ON condition#Not quite. A join needs an ON condition that follows the key.
~orders LEFT JOIN customers, then remove orders#Not quite. The task needs the orders shown, and an inner join already gives the right rows.
}
